from a1 import t
C6='Life, family and the world'
t('marriage-divorce', C6, 'Marriage, divorce and annulment', 'The Church bans divorce, and annulment is “Catholic divorce”.',
 ["Jesus taught that marriage is a lifelong union: “What God has joined together, let not man put asunder” (Mark 10:2-12; Matthew 19:3-9). A valid sacramental marriage between baptised persons cannot be dissolved by any human power. The Church can recognise that a marriage which looked valid never truly existed because something essential was lacking from the beginning, such as free consent, or the intention to be faithful, or the ability to carry out the commitment of marriage. That is what an annulment declares: it does not dissolve a marriage; it is a finding that there was none.",
  "Civil divorce is sometimes tolerated for protection, but remarriage after divorce while the first spouse is alive is not possible for Catholics, since “whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery” (Luke 16:18). Divorced Catholics who have not remarried remain full members of the Church, and those who have remarried can still take part in the life of the Church in many ways."],
 [('Mark 10:2-12','What God has joined together, let no one put asunder'),('Matthew 19:3-9','Moses allowed divorce because of the hardness of your hearts'),('Ephesians 5:25-32','Christ and the Church: a great mystery'),('Malachi 2:13-16','I hate divorce, says the Lord')],
 [1601,1602,1638,1639,1640,1641,1642,1643,1644,1645,1646,1647,1648,1649,1650,1651],['Marriage','Divorce','Annulment'],[])

t('contraception', C6, 'Contraception', 'The Church’s ban on contraception is out of date.',
 ["The Church teaches that marriage’s sexual union has two inseparable meanings: love between the spouses (unitive) and openness to new life (procreative). Deliberately blocking one of these separates what God joined together (Humanae Vitae 12, 1968). The Church does not demand that couples have as many children as possible: it teaches “responsible parenthood”, which includes spacing births for serious reasons, using natural family planning (NFP), which works with the body’s natural cycles of fertility. NFP differs from contraception in the means used and in the openness to life that remains in every act.",
  "The Bible condemns the deliberate frustrating of conception in Onan’s case (Genesis 38:9-10), although scholars debate the main point of that story. The tradition, from the Fathers, taught that contraception is wrong. Humanae Vitae predicted social consequences, which many see in today’s culture. The Church’s teaching is demanding, and the Church offers pastoral care, mercy in Confession, and help with NFP."],
 [('Genesis 1:27-28','Be fruitful and multiply'),('Genesis 38:8-10','Onan and the wasted seed'),('Psalm 127:3-5','Children are a heritage from the Lord'),('Ephesians 5:25-33','Love as Christ loved the Church')],
 [2366,2367,2368,2369,2370,2371,2372],['Contraception','NFP','Humanae Vitae'],['marriage-divorce','abortion'])

t('abortion', C6, 'Abortion', 'The Bible does not mention abortion.',
 ["The Church has always taught that human life must be respected from conception to natural death. The Bible does not use the word “abortion”, but it speaks of God’s knowledge of persons before birth: “Before I formed you in the womb I knew you” (Jeremiah 1:5), “you knit me together in my mother’s womb” (Psalm 139:13-16), and John the Baptist “leaped for joy” in Elizabeth’s womb (Luke 1:41-44), where Luke uses the same word, brephos, for the unborn child as for the baby in the manger (Luke 2:12). The commandment “You shall not kill” (Exodus 20:13) protects the innocent.",
  "The Didache (about AD 100) says, “You shall not kill a child by abortion nor kill it once it is born.” The Church also asks us to care for women in difficult pregnancies, and to offer them real help. For those who have had an abortion, the Church offers mercy through the sacrament of Confession and through healing programs such as Project Rachel."],
 [('Jeremiah 1:5','Before I formed you in the womb I knew you'),('Psalm 139:13-16','You knit me together in my mother’s womb'),('Luke 1:39-44','The baby leaped in her womb'),('Exodus 20:13','You shall not kill'),('Isaiah 49:1','The Lord called me from the womb')],
 [2270,2271,2272,2273,2274,2275,2276,2277],['Abortion','Life','Pro-life'],['contraception'])

t('science-faith', C6, 'Faith, science and evolution', 'Science has disproved the Bible; Genesis contradicts evolution.',
 ["The Church teaches that faith and reason cannot contradict each other, since the same God is the author of both (CCC 159). Genesis does not teach how the heavens go, but how one goes to heaven (as Galileo quoted Cardinal Baronius). Genesis 1-3 teaches in the language of its time, with a literary structure of days and a garden, that God created everything out of nothing and called it good, that human beings are made in his image, and that sin entered the world through a free choice. The Church does not require anyone to believe in a six-day creation or a young earth.",
  "The Church has not condemned evolution as a scientific theory. Pope Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) allowed it as a matter for discussion, and Pope John Paul II said it was “more than a hypothesis” (1996), while insisting that the human soul is created directly by God. The Big Bang theory was first proposed by a Catholic priest, Georges Lemaître. The Church’s teaching is that the universe has a Creator, and human beings have a spiritual soul."],
 [('Genesis 1:1-2:3','In the beginning God created'),('Psalm 19:1','The heavens declare the glory of God'),('Wisdom 13:1-9','From the greatness of created things comes a corresponding perception of their Creator'),('Romans 1:19-20','His invisible nature has been perceived in the things that have been made')],
 [159,282,283,284,285,286,337,355,356,357,362,366],['Creation','Evolution','Science'],[])

t('suffering', C6, 'Why does God allow suffering?', 'If God is good and all-powerful, why is there evil and suffering?',
 ["The Catechism says plainly that there is no quick answer: “Only Christian faith as a whole constitutes the answer to this question” (CCC 309). God made a world “in a state of journeying” and gave free will to angels and humans, and with it the possibility of moral evil. He permits evil only because he can bring a greater good from it, as with the cross, the worst evil in history, which became the source of our salvation. The Book of Job shows that God does not give a neat explanation for the suffering of the innocent, but meets Job in person.",
  "Christians do not say suffering is good in itself. Jesus wept at the tomb of Lazarus (John 11:35), healed the sick, and promised that God “will wipe away every tear” (Revelation 21:4). He also shares our suffering: on the cross God entered the worst we suffer. Paul says our sufferings can be joined to Christ’s for the good of others (Colossians 1:24) and that “all things work together for good for those who love God” (Romans 8:28)."],
 [('Job 38:1-42:6','God answers Job'),('Romans 8:18-28','Sufferings of this present time; all things work for good'),('Revelation 21:1-5','He will wipe away every tear'),('John 11:33-35','Jesus wept'),('Genesis 50:20','You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good'),('Colossians 1:24','Rejoicing in my sufferings for your sake')],
 [309,310,311,312,313,314,324,385,1521,1522,1523,1524,1525,1526],['Suffering','Evil','Providence','Theodicy'],['hell'])
